<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Chios</h1><p class="st-meta">1304 CE – 1329 CE · capital Chios</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Genoese lordship of the Zaccaria family on Chios, granted by Byzantium to defend the island against Turkish raiders and lost to a Greek rising in 1329.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-zaccaria">Benedetto</a> I <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a>, a Genoese admiral and merchant who already held the rich alum mines of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phocaea">Phocaea</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chios">Chios</a> in 1304 to protect it from Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II</a>, unable to defend the island himself, recognised Zaccaria rule for a fixed term, which was later renewed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a> ruled as nominal Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, but in practice as independent lords. They profited from the island's mastic, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and from alum trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-zaccaria">Martino Zaccaria</a> built a fleet that fought Turkish raiders successfully, took the title King and Despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> pretenders, and struck his own coins.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-zaccaria">Martino</a>'s growing independence alarmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. In 1329 the Chiots rose against him with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III</a>, who landed with a fleet; Martino was captured and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chios">Chios</a> returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. The Genoese came back in 1346, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maona-of-chios">Maona of Chios</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-chios.jpg" alt="Lordship of Chios" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Genoa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1304 CE – 1329 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chios</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Benedetto I Zaccaria, Benedetto II Zaccaria, Martino Zaccaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Genoese Ligurian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zaccaria Chios</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=lordship-of-chios" aria-label="Lordship of Chios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5330 800 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Chios lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5650 1056L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5650 1056h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5650 1056h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chios. <a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=lordship-of-chios">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

